 19th we  went directly to Washington and called  on the Secretary of State at once to  proceed with our deal the way the  business was done certainly illustrates  our government of those days it would be  supposed that before we sailed from  Japan the mission would have had a  receipt for the $800,000 that had been  duly paid but in reality there was  hardly anything that could be recognized  as an official voucher our files  included some 10 pieces of paper with  simple notations of $50,000 $100,000 etc  but there was no statement of how or for  what purpose mr. prawn had received the  money among the papers were several  oddly shaped sheets with nothing but the  amounts and the name Prue you inscribed  the American parties might easily so  inclined have assumed a skeptical air  and refused to honor such pieces of  paper  there was much discussion among the  members of the mission before the voyage  but they decided finally that this  apparent neglect could be made a proof  of friendship between the two countries  Japan had trusted the American Minister  rather than the Japanese government had  put its faith in the goodwill of the  American government and no formal  agreement was deemed necessary the  papers were simply memoranda in which we  would not place any importance having  decided upon this attitude which was due  to the force of circumstance when we  opened the negotiations we were greeted  at once by mr. Peron himself without any  hesitation or formality he wanted to  know if we would take a ship or have the  surplus fund returned the mission felt  relieved at this fine attitude of the  American ex-minister we were taken on a  tour of inspection and finally decided  on a steel craft named the stone wall  later called by us as Zuma Kahn then we  purchased several hundred or several  thousand rifles still some seventy or  eighty thousand dollars of our fund  remained and this we left in the care of  the American government it was not until  the following year the first year of the  meiji era that the ship reached japan  then with the new year in early January  came the Battle of Russia me the defeat  of the Shogun's army and the retreat  back to Edo of Shogun Yoshi Nobu himself  again great confusion swept the country  and this marked the beginning of the  Imperial restoration though I did not  engage in politics I was not entirely  ignorant of the political world on the  voyage to Europe I used to talk over the  problems of the time with the other  interpreters man Suki and Mr curry what  do you think of it I said one day the  Shogunate cannot hold the country  together much longer it seems to me that  all the clans might get together and  form a federation like Germany what'd he  say to this idea Matt Suki and Mizuki  agreed with me that this would be the  most peaceful solution to the crisis  then going on to talk about our own  careers I said if I should say what I'd  like to do if I could I would like to  become tutor to the big chief with a  salary of 200 bales of rice a year and  the chance of teaching him all the new  ideas of civilization and bringing about  a great Reformation to the country  [Music]  but to return to the contemporary  situation in the country I noticed when  I looked around that all the ronin  adventurers and so-called Patriots and  collected in Kyoto around the Imperial  course the Shogunate in Edo on the other  hand was trying to keep its own as the  central government against this rising  power these two political forces had  come to be called Kiner the supporters  of the Emperor and Sabah ku the  supporters of the Shogun if I may sum up  my position between these two sides  number one I disliked the bureaucratic  oppressive conservative ante foreign  policy of the Shogunate so I could not  side with it  but two yet the followers of the  imperial cause were still more  anti-foreign and more violent in their  action so I had even less sympathy with  them number three  regardless of right or wrong an  ambitious man might cast his lot with  one or other of the parties to win a  place for himself for in a troubled time  one can best do a big thing but there  was no such desire in me  [Music]  another instance of the stubborn  conservatism of an official may be  pertinent here I was reading chambers  book on economics when I spoke of the  book to a certain high official in the  Treasury Bureau one day he became much  interested and wanted me to show him a  translation I had once began translating  it when I came upon the word competition  which there was no equivalent in  Japanese after trying several words I  was obliged to use an invention of my  own Keo so literally race fight when the  officials saw my translation he appeared  much impressed then he said suddenly  here is a word fight what does it mean  it's such a non peaceful word that is  nothing new I replied that is exactly  what all Japanese merchants are doing if  one merchant begins to sell things cheap  his neighbor will try to sell them even  cheaper or if one merchant improves his  merchandise to attract more buyers  another will try to take the trade from  him by offering goods of still better  quality thus all merchants race and  fight and this is the way money values  are fixed I understand now but don't you  think there is too much effort in  Western Affairs consider that too much  effort all the commerce of the world is  conductive fundamentally like that yes  perhaps went on the official I  understand the idea but that word fight  is not conductive to peace I would not  take the paper with that word to the  chancellor for his judgment from the  gentleman's exception to my rendering of  the term I suppose he would rather have  seen such phrases men being kite to each  other in a book on economics doubtless  the official would have approved a book  that advocated man's loyalty to his Lord  a love of the nation shown in connection  with trade open generosity from a  merchant in times of national stress etc  from this little incident one may gather  the character of the other officials  whom he represented in the government  when the invading army did come however  it proved to be very well disciplined  and no unreasonable violence was ever  done peace and a stable government  established in April of that year my  school structure was finished  [Music]  it may see that I was unreasonably  severe in hatred of the government but  the whole reason of my stand was that I  believed the new government to be  carrying out the ancient policy of  exclusiveness  and antagonism against Western culture  and I feared that the change from  Shogunate to Imperial regime would bring  no good to the country I have never told  anyone of the dire helpless state of my  mind at that time but I will confess it  now watching the unfortunate condition  of the country I feared in reality that  we might not be able to hold our own  against foreign aggressiveness yet no  one in all the land was there with whom  I could talk over my anxiety no one  anywhere east west north or south as I  searched whom I could depend on as I  look back today over thirty years since  it all seems like a dream how advanced  and secure the country is now I can do  nothing but bless with a full heart this  glorious enlightenment of Japan today  [Music]  [Music]

